{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017h8TYKa3rRgDCLapLzQMPk","type":"tool_result","content":"84:**7.** `kubectl logs <pod> -c cache`. Without `-c`, the command has to guess, and on a multi-container Pod it will refuse or pick the first container rather than the one you meant. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 §2 — multi-container Pods]*\n124:The stakes, plainly. Failure signatures are the most likely troubleshooting material on the KCNA exam, and they are also the material most study guides reduce to a two-column glossary: signature on the left, one-line cause on the right. A glossary will get you through a question that names a string. It will not get you through a question that describes a symptom and asks what you would check, which is the shape the exam actually favors and the shape this chapter is built for.\n200:This is the largest failure family, the one the exam reaches for most often, and the one where the method pays off hardest. Almost every signature in it produces the same visible symptom, a Pod that is not running, from a completely different cause.\n235:If a Pod was rejected at admission, by Pod Security Admission, by a validating webhook, by a quota, **there is no Pod object to describe.** The API server refused the write. Chapter 12 already flagged this to you and said it \"shows up at a different point in the triage flow\"; here is that point.\n265:An unbound PersistentVolumeClaim belongs in this family too. A Pod that mounts a PVC cannot be scheduled until that claim binds, so a storage problem arrives disguised as a scheduling problem: a Pod sitting in `Pending` for reasons that have nothing to do with CPU, memory, or taints. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 11 §2 — PV and PVC binding]*\n305:This one is a favorite on exams because the symptom is so far from the cause.\n319:If a Pod's containers show `PodInitializing`, an init container is still running, or is failing. Init containers run to completion, in order, before any app container starts, so a single init container that exits non-zero and gets restarted will hold the entire Pod at the gate indefinitely.\n341:`kubectl config current-context` costs you one second and eliminates the whole category.\n431:  SCOPE      kubectl config current-context      Right cluster? Right namespace?\n435:    │        kubectl get pod <name> -o wide      Which node? Which IP?\n528:If Pod creation was refused at admission, there is no Pod object to describe, and the refusal message goes to the controller that attempted the create: the ReplicaSet.\n614:Both mean \"something killed your workload for using too much memory.\" Everything else about them is different, and the exam knows it.\n629:  Reason: OOMKilled                      Reason: Evicted\n805:crictl ps        # containers the runtime is actually running, on this node\n809:The argument is the whole point: **when the cluster's view and the node's view disagree, `crictl` is how you see the node's view.** A container that `kubectl` cannot see but `crictl ps` lists is a container the kubelet failed to register, which localizes your problem to the kubelet, not to the workload. A container that neither can see was never started, which is a different problem entirely.\n815:[Omitted long matching line]\n854:**4.** You run `kubectl get pods` on a node you suspect is broken and see nothing unexpected. You then run `crictl ps` on that node and find a container running that `kubectl` did not report. What does this tell you?\n959:[Omitted long matching line]\n1046:The same absence has a second consequence people meet separately and never connect: **a HorizontalPodAutoscaler reads the same Metrics API.** [source: k8s-docs-resource-metrics-pipeline-2026-08-31] An HPA on a cluster without metrics-server will be created successfully, will report unknown metrics, and will never scale anything: the same pattern, one layer up. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §2 — the HPA in one sentence]* and *[cross-bearing: see Ch 17 §7 — the autoscaling landscape]*\n1048:One scope note that the exam likes: metrics-server \"is meant only for autoscaling purposes — for example, don't use it to forward metrics to monitoring solutions, or as a source of monitoring solution metrics.\" [source: k8s-docs-resource-metrics-pipeline-2026-08-23] It holds current values for autoscaling decisions. It is not a monitoring system, it keeps no history, and you cannot query it for what happened an hour ago. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 18 §3 — metrics-server versus a monitoring system]*\n1066:The most common answer is an agent on every node that ships logs to a backend: a node-level logging agent, typically run as a DaemonSet so that exactly one lands on each node. That is the whole gloss you need here; the agents themselves, and the architecture around them, are Chapter 18's. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 18 §6 — node-level logging agents]* and *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §7 — DaemonSets]*\n1258:1. **The never-started / started-then-died split, and which signature belongs to which.** This is the chapter in one line, and it is the discrimination the exam reaches for most. `Pending`, the image-pull family, and `CreateContainerConfigError` are never-started. `CrashLoopBackOff`, `OOMKilled`, and `Evicted` are started-then-died. The restart count tells you which side you are on.\n1285:Fifteen questions, weighted toward the two signature families. Several present a symptom and ask what you would *do*, rather than naming a string, which is the shape the exam favors and the shape a glossary cannot answer.\n1317:A) The kubelet evicts the whole Pod; phase becomes `Failed` with `Reason: Evicted`\n1364:**12.** On a node you suspect of a kubelet fault, `kubectl get pods -o wide` shows two Pods on that node. `crictl ps` on the node shows four running containers belonging to Kubernetes workloads. What does the discrepancy indicate?\n1461:**12. A.** This is a counting question disguised as a diagnostic one. `crictl ps` lists **containers**; `kubectl get pods` lists **Pods**. Two Pods with two containers each is four containers, and everything is consistent."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed863541-8a1f-4f92-a038-f7da00387088","uuid":"84f23a66-3e52-45d8-a7a0-614b489c3f94","timestamp":"2026-08-31T07:17:53.184Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"84:**7.** `kubectl logs <pod> -c cache`. Without `-c`, the command has to guess, and on a multi-container Pod it will refuse or pick the first container rather than the one you meant. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 §2 — multi-container Pods]*\n124:The stakes, plainly. Failure signatures are the most likely troubleshooting material on the KCNA exam, and they are also the material most study guides reduce to a two-column glossary: signature on the left, one-line cause on the right. A glossary will get you through a question that names a string. It will not get you through a question that describes a symptom and asks what you would check, which is the shape the exam actually favors and the shape this chapter is built for.\n200:This is the largest failure family, the one the exam reaches for most often, and the one where the method pays off hardest. Almost every signature in it produces the same visible symptom, a Pod that is not running, from a completely different cause.\n235:If a Pod was rejected at admission, by Pod Security Admission, by a validating webhook, by a quota, **there is no Pod object to describe.** The API server refused the write. Chapter 12 already flagged this to you and said it \"shows up at a different point in the triage flow\"; here is that point.\n265:An unbound PersistentVolumeClaim belongs in this family too. A Pod that mounts a PVC cannot be scheduled until that claim binds, so a storage problem arrives disguised as a scheduling problem: a Pod sitting in `Pending` for reasons that have nothing to do with CPU, memory, or taints. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 11 §2 — PV and PVC binding]*\n305:This one is a favorite on exams because the symptom is so far from the cause.\n319:If a Pod's containers show `PodInitializing`, an init container is still running, or is failing. Init containers run to completion, in order, before any app container starts, so a single init container that exits non-zero and gets restarted will hold the entire Pod at the gate indefinitely.\n341:`kubectl config current-context` costs you one second and eliminates the whole category.\n431:  SCOPE      kubectl config current-context      Right cluster? Right namespace?\n435:    │        kubectl get pod <name> -o wide      Which node? Which IP?\n528:If Pod creation was refused at admission, there is no Pod object to describe, and the refusal message goes to the controller that attempted the create: the ReplicaSet.\n614:Both mean \"something killed your workload for using too much memory.\" Everything else about them is different, and the exam knows it.\n629:  Reason: OOMKilled                      Reason: Evicted\n805:crictl ps        # containers the runtime is actually running, on this node\n809:The argument is the whole point: **when the cluster's view and the node's view disagree, `crictl` is how you see the node's view.** A container that `kubectl` cannot see but `crictl ps` lists is a container the kubelet failed to register, which localizes your problem to the kubelet, not to the workload. A container that neither can see was never started, which is a different problem entirely.\n815:[Omitted long matching line]\n854:**4.** You run `kubectl get pods` on a node you suspect is broken and see nothing unexpected. You then run `crictl ps` on that node and find a container running that `kubectl` did not report. What does this tell you?\n959:[Omitted long matching line]\n1046:The same absence has a second consequence people meet separately and never connect: **a HorizontalPodAutoscaler reads the same Metrics API.** [source: k8s-docs-resource-metrics-pipeline-2026-08-31] An HPA on a cluster without metrics-server will be created successfully, will report unknown metrics, and will never scale anything: the same pattern, one layer up. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §2 — the HPA in one sentence]* and *[cross-bearing: see Ch 17 §7 — the autoscaling landscape]*\n1048:One scope note that the exam likes: metrics-server \"is meant only for autoscaling purposes — for example, don't use it to forward metrics to monitoring solutions, or as a source of monitoring solution metrics.\" [source: k8s-docs-resource-metrics-pipeline-2026-08-23] It holds current values for autoscaling decisions. It is not a monitoring system, it keeps no history, and you cannot query it for what happened an hour ago. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 18 §3 — metrics-server versus a monitoring system]*\n1066:The most common answer is an agent on every node that ships logs to a backend: a node-level logging agent, typically run as a DaemonSet so that exactly one lands on each node. That is the whole gloss you need here; the agents themselves, and the architecture around them, are Chapter 18's. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 18 §6 — node-level logging agents]* and *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §7 — DaemonSets]*\n1258:1. **The never-started / started-then-died split, and which signature belongs to which.** This is the chapter in one line, and it is the discrimination the exam reaches for most. `Pending`, the image-pull family, and `CreateContainerConfigError` are never-started. `CrashLoopBackOff`, `OOMKilled`, and `Evicted` are started-then-died. The restart count tells you which side you are on.\n1285:Fifteen questions, weighted toward the two signature families. Several present a symptom and ask what you would *do*, rather than naming a string, which is the shape the exam favors and the shape a glossary cannot answer.\n1317:A) The kubelet evicts the whole Pod; phase becomes `Failed` with `Reason: Evicted`\n1364:**12.** On a node you suspect of a kubelet fault, `kubectl get pods -o wide` shows two Pods on that node. `crictl ps` on the node shows four running containers belonging to Kubernetes workloads. What does the discrepancy indicate?\n1461:**12. A.** This is a counting question disguised as a diagnostic one. `crictl ps` lists **containers**; `kubectl get pods` lists **Pods**. Two Pods with two containers each is four containers, and everything is consistent.","numLines":26,"totalLines":26}}
