{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CyvxfNmd4nDYU1DGCtL9LQ","type":"tool_result","content":"72:**1.** Container state. `Reason` is a field on the container state, not on the Pod phase. The five Pod phases are `Pending`, `Running`, `Succeeded`, `Failed`, `Unknown`, and none of them is called `ContainerCreating`. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 §5 — Pod phases and container states]*\n647:If a container is OOM-killed repeatedly, you will see it as `CrashLoopBackOff` in `kubectl get pods`. The crash loop is the *visible* signature, and `OOMKilled` is the reason underneath it, visible in `describe` under the container's last state. That layering trips people up: the two are not alternatives, they are two altitudes of the same event.\n651:**Out Of Memory**, since the acronym has now appeared twice without being spelled out. It is a kernel concept, not a Kubernetes one; Kubernetes is only reporting what the kernel did.\n661:[Omitted long matching line]\n806:crictl logs <id> # that container's logs, read from the runtime directly\n815:[Omitted long matching line]\n1066:The most common answer is an agent on every node that ships logs to a backend: a node-level logging agent, typically run as a DaemonSet so that exactly one lands on each node. That is the whole gloss you need here; the agents themselves, and the architecture around them, are Chapter 18's. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 18 §6 — node-level logging agents]* and *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §7 — DaemonSets]*"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed863541-8a1f-4f92-a038-f7da00387088","uuid":"c32e7cad-11c2-4363-9fcf-d52f9c1940ca","timestamp":"2026-08-31T07:18:53.643Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"72:**1.** Container state. `Reason` is a field on the container state, not on the Pod phase. The five Pod phases are `Pending`, `Running`, `Succeeded`, `Failed`, `Unknown`, and none of them is called `ContainerCreating`. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 §5 — Pod phases and container states]*\n647:If a container is OOM-killed repeatedly, you will see it as `CrashLoopBackOff` in `kubectl get pods`. The crash loop is the *visible* signature, and `OOMKilled` is the reason underneath it, visible in `describe` under the container's last state. That layering trips people up: the two are not alternatives, they are two altitudes of the same event.\n651:**Out Of Memory**, since the acronym has now appeared twice without being spelled out. It is a kernel concept, not a Kubernetes one; Kubernetes is only reporting what the kernel did.\n661:[Omitted long matching line]\n806:crictl logs <id> # that container's logs, read from the runtime directly\n815:[Omitted long matching line]\n1066:The most common answer is an agent on every node that ships logs to a backend: a node-level logging agent, typically run as a DaemonSet so that exactly one lands on each node. That is the whole gloss you need here; the agents themselves, and the architecture around them, are Chapter 18's. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 18 §6 — node-level logging agents]* and *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §7 — DaemonSets]*","numLines":7,"totalLines":7}}
